TRAILER_HELP = """\
\b
Trailer form (the last run of line comments in the file, keys in this exact order):
\b
    // PORT STATUS
    // source: src/libslic3r/Geometry/Circle.cpp
    // source: src/libslic3r/Geometry/Circle.hpp
    // confidence: high
    // tier: exact
    // oracle: built
\b
"source:" may repeat (one .cpp/.hpp pair, or a header-only unit) and every path must exist in
the vendored tree at the pin and have a row in port/manifest.tsv naming this module.
"confidence:" is one of high, medium, low. "tier:" must equal that row's "equality tier" cell
verbatim. "oracle:" is one of release, built -- the two oracles the spec names, spelled as
port/reports/oracle-parity.tsv's "normative oracle" column spells them.
"""

NUMERIC_RULE_HELP = """\
\b
Numeric-constant rule (R14, R16): a line with a numeric literal fails unless every non-exempt
literal on it is covered by a "// PORT SRC: <vendored path>:<line>" marker (on that line or a
contiguous comment line immediately above), or every literal on the line is exempt. More than
one marker may cover a line; each marker must match at least one literal on the line it covers,
and every non-exempt literal must be matched by at least one covering marker's cited line.
\b
Exempt literal set (closed): the integers 0, 1 and 2 and their negatives -- structural indices,
arities and strides, not transcribed constants. Every other literal, including every float,
needs a marker.
\b
Exempt positions (closed): inside a line or block comment; inside a string or char literal;
inside an attribute such as #[...]; inside a test file under crates/*/tests/ or a #[cfg(test)]
module.
\b
Both sets are closed. There is no per-module, per-file or per-line opt-out: no marker, no
allowlist file and no comment suppresses this rule. Noise on first contact with a real module
is answered by a pull request against port/port-status, never by the module.
"""


MARKER_WHITELIST_HELP = """\
\b
Marker whitelist (closed): every port marker is one of TODO(port):, PERF(port):, PORT NOTE:,
SAFETY:, PORT SRC: or FIDELITY(port):. A line comment that looks like a marker (an all-capitals
word, optionally "(port)", then a colon -- or, case-insensitively, the exact spelling of one of
the six) but spells none of these six exactly is a finding -- e.g. "// TODO:", "// Todo(port):"
or "// PORT-SRC:". Ordinary prose such as "// Returns:" is never a candidate and never flagged.
\b
Exempt positions (closed): this check does not run at all on a line inside a #[cfg(test)]
module body, nor on an attribute line such as #[inline] -- the same two positions marked exempt
for the numeric-literal rule above. Inside them nothing is examined, so the exemption is wider
than the "// PROOF:" convention it exists for: a near-miss spelling written there is not
flagged ("// TODO:" inside a #[cfg(test)] block is not a finding), and a recognised marker
written there is not added to the per-marker counts printed above ("// PORT NOTE:" there prints
as PORT NOTE=0). The printed count therefore excludes test-module markers, a deliberate
narrowing of R14's "prints the count per marker kind" chosen over widening R14's closed
six-marker list to a seventh spelling. It is consistent with the convention's other test
position, a file under crates/<crate>/tests/, which this script's own glob
(crates/*/src/**/*.rs) never reads and whose markers were never counted either.
\b
That exemption belongs to this check alone and is not uniform across the script: an "unsafe"
block inside a #[cfg(test)] module still needs its "// SAFETY:" comment, and a malformed
"// PERF(port):" or "// FIDELITY(port):" written there is still a finding (those rules are
exempt-unaware), while the counts above print those same markers as 0.
\b
"// PROOF: <path>" (the proof-presence convention, see below) is not a port marker and is never
whitelisted or counted. Both of its sanctioned forms sit in the positions above -- a
#[cfg(test)] module, exempt here, or a file under crates/<crate>/tests/, which this script
never reads -- so neither form widens the six-marker whitelist R14 states. Anywhere else in a
ported module it is an ordinary unrecognised-marker finding like any other near miss: the
exemption is the position, not the spelling.
"""

GATE_HELP = """\
\b
Status, the gate and the two modes (R14, R16, R19, R26, R29): every port/manifest.tsv row's
status is derived from the Rust tree, never read from its own "status" cell -- "ported" needs a
mod declaration reaching the target file, no todo!()/unimplemented!(), and a "// PROOF: <proof
path>" marker beside a #[test] naming the module; "drafted" is everything short of that with
either a PORT STATUS trailer (no mod line yet) or a mod line with no proof; anything else is
"not-started". --per-commit runs every check above plus the required-fuzz-target check (a
"required" row that is "ported" needs crates/slic3r-ffi/fuzz/fuzz_targets/<crate>_<module>.rs,
that exact path) and the two document-section checks (R12/R13/R29, docs/PORTING.md and
docs/WORKFLOW.md's exact headings) -- and passes while a row is "not-started" or "drafted". Only
the bare command adds the gate: the burn-down (todo!(), unimplemented!(), modules gated out of
the build) at zero and every in-scope row "ported". Both modes print the burn-down, the
remaining-row count and the "unknown" (and unclassified) port/ownership.tsv row counts, and a
line stating that status above judges a proof's presence, never its outcome -- cargo test
--workspace is what actually runs a proof; only the bare command fails on the burn-down and the
remaining-row count. Exits non-zero before any of this, in either mode, if port/manifest.tsv is
missing or its header lacks a column status derivation or the gate reads, since zero in-scope
rows derived from a broken manifest must never read as the port being complete.
"""

PR_BODY_HELP = """\
